# Objective
Write the SQL migration files to create the babies table.

The idea behind the SQL migration files is that you essentially can version control the changes that you make to your db.
- up will be the applied changes, or "migration"
- down will undo those changes

This way db changes are traced and repeatable.

The babies table is constructed as such:
- id (uuid) PK - unique id to identify a baby. UUID can be automatically created using the pgcrypto extension's gen_random_uuid() to provide the default 
- full_name (TEXT) - name of baby, required via NOT NULL
- birth_date (DATE) - optional 
- created_at (TIMESTAMPTZ) - created date for the record. default value is the current time stamp, must be non null if user specified
- create a constraint on full name that it cannot be a string of whitespace

the general structure of the create statement here is 
CREATE TABLE [IF NOT EXISTS] table_name (
    col_name col_type column_constraints
    ....
    table_constraints
)


==================
migrations for create diaper events

diaper_events is modeled as:
- id UUID PK with auto UUID creation via gen_random_uuid()
- baby_ID UUID FK that references babies' ID. comes with a constraint for referential integrity
- occurred_at TIMESTAMPTZ non nullable
- created_at TIMESTAMPTZ non nullable with default of current TIMESTAMPTZ
- diaper_type TEXT that must be in the list of valid types (wet, dry, mixed) checked with constraint
- notes varchar of 500 length, nullable

for this one, we created an index to support the query pattern of diapers by baby id ordered by recency
CREATE INDEX idx_babies_diaper_events ON diaper_events (baby_id, occurred_at DESC);
this will create the index on the multi column baby_id and in DESC order by occurred_at
